Now I have the full picture. Here's everything:

RabbitMQ Dashboard http://localhost:15672 — login: guest / guest

What to look at:

Exchanges tab → find pharmacy.exchange (TopicExchange)
Queues tab → find notification.queue — shows messages ready/delivered
Click the queue → you can manually publish a test message or see message rates
How notifications are triggered

The flow is: order-service places an order → publishes to pharmacy.exchange with routing key notification.send → notification-service consumes from notification.queue and logs it.

To trigger it, place an order via Swagger:

http://localhost:9091/swagger-ui/index.html → register + login → copy JWT
http://localhost:9093/swagger-ui/index.html → Authorize with Bearer <token> → call POST /api/orders
Then check notification-service logs:
docker logs notification-service -f
You'll see something like:

[NOTIFICATION] type=ORDER_PLACED to=user@email.com subject=...
And in RabbitMQ dashboard under notification.queue you'll see the message count tick up and back down as it's consumed.

To see live notification logs just run:

docker logs notification-service -f


Step 1 — Login to Docker Hub:

docker login
Enter your Docker Hub username and password.

Step 2 — Build JARs:

mvn clean package -DskipTests
Step 3 — Build and tag all images (replace yourusername with your Docker Hub username):

docker build -t yourusername/eureka-server:latest ./eureka-server
docker build -t yourusername/gateway-service:latest ./gateway-service
docker build -t yourusername/auth-service:latest ./auth-service
docker build -t yourusername/catalog-service:latest ./catalog-service
docker build -t yourusername/order-service:latest ./order-service
docker build -t yourusername/admin-service:latest ./admin-service
docker build -t yourusername/payment-service:latest ./payment-service
docker build -t yourusername/notification-service:latest ./notification-service
Step 4 — Push all images:

docker push yourusername/eureka-server:latest
docker push yourusername/gateway-service:latest
docker push yourusername/auth-service:latest
docker push yourusername/catalog-service:latest
docker push yourusername/order-service:latest
docker push yourusername/admin-service:latest
docker push yourusername/payment-service:latest
docker push yourusername/notification-service:latest
Or do it all in one script — run this in PowerShell replacing yourusername:


$user = "yourusername"
$services = @("eureka-server","gateway-service","auth-service","catalog-service","order-service","admin-service","payment-service","notification-service")
foreach ($svc in $services) {
    docker build -t "$user/$svc`:latest" "./$svc"
    docker push "$user/$svc`:latest"
}ocker push yourusername/notification-service:latest
